# Version DOI (reserved on the draft record; resolves once the record is
# published). This is the specific-version doi, not the concept doi: the concept
# doi follows the newest version and would unpin the reported numbers from the
# archive they were computed against.
keywords:
  - microservices
  - distributed tracing
  - OpenTelemetry
  - application logs
  - security anomaly detection
  - server-side request forgery
  - labelled dataset
  - Train-Ticket
  - TeaStore
abstract: >-
  A labelled corpus of OpenTelemetry distributed traces joined to application
  logs, for evaluating security-anomaly detection in microservice systems. Five
  CWE-referenced scenarios (server-side request forgery, credential abuse,
  injection, data exfiltration, resource exhaustion) are collected on two
  architecturally distinct benchmarks: Train-Ticket under Kubernetes and
  TeaStore under Docker Compose. Every request-level trace carries a normal or
  attack label and a four-state log observation status, so an absent log join
  is distinguishable from an absent log record. The corpus holds 11,000
  labelled trace records: 5,000 on Train-Ticket and 6,000 on TeaStore. It ships
  with a 120-cell demonstrative detector registry, a fold manifest, and audit
  tables that record which cells are interpretable and which are withdrawn.
